==================================================================
DAY 15 - PART B: LAB
Pod IPs, routes, and veth pairs
Time: 75-90 minutes
==================================================================

mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week3 && cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week3


STEP 1: Run pods on different nodes
-----------------------------------
k create deploy net --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=4
k get pods -l app=net -o wide

Note each pod's IP and NODE. Pods on the same node share a /24.


STEP 2: Find the pod ranges
---------------------------
k get nodes -o custom-columns=NODE:.metadata.name,PODCIDR:.spec.podCIDR,IP:.status.addresses[0].address
k cluster-info dump | grep -m1 -E "cluster-cidr|service-cluster-ip-range"


STEP 3: Pod to pod (same node and different node)
-------------------------------------------------
k run tool --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- sleep infinity
k get pod tool -o wide
k get pods -l app=net -o wide

k exec tool -- curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" <IP-of-a-net-pod-on-SAME-node>
k exec tool -- curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" <IP-of-a-net-pod-on-OTHER-node>

Both return 200. No NAT, no port mapping.

Trace the path:
  k exec tool -- traceroute -n <IP-on-other-node>


STEP 4: Inside the pod's network namespace
------------------------------------------
k exec tool -- ip addr show eth0
k exec tool -- ip route

The pod sees one interface (eth0) and a default route via its node.


STEP 5: On the node: veth pairs and routes
------------------------------------------
NODE=$(k get pod tool -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}')
docker exec $NODE ip addr | grep -A1 veth | head
docker exec $NODE ip route

You will see routes like:
  10.244.1.0/24 via 172.18.0.3 dev eth0     <- pods on another node
  10.244.2.5 dev vethXXXX scope host        <- a local pod

This is kindnet doing simple routing (no overlay).


STEP 6: Match a pod to its veth
-------------------------------
k exec tool -- cat /sys/class/net/eth0/iflink
  -> prints a number N
docker exec $NODE ip -o link | grep "^N:"
That veth on the node is the other end of the pod's eth0.


STEP 7: Capture traffic between pods
------------------------------------
Terminal 2:
  k exec tool -- tcpdump -ni eth0 port 80 -c 6
Terminal 1:
  k exec tool -- curl -s <IP-of-a-net-pod> >/dev/null

Source and destination are the real pod IPs: proof of "no NAT".


STEP 8: Watch IPs change
------------------------
k get pods -l app=net -o wide
k delete pod -l app=net
k get pods -l app=net -o wide

All new IPs. That is why we need Services tomorrow.


STEP 9: Clean up and commit
---------------------------
k delete deploy net
k delete pod tool
cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day15: network model"
